[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Some integral numbers are odd. All are more odd, or less odd, than others.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Even numbers satisfy `n = 2m` ( with `m` also integral ) and we will ( completely arbitrarily ) think of odd numbers as `n = 2m + 1`.  
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Now, some odd numbers can be more odd than others: when for some `n`, `m` is more odd than for another's. Recursively. :]  
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Even numbers are always less odd than odd numbers, but they also can be more, or less, odd than other even numbers, by the same mechanism.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m # Task
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Given a _non-empty_ finite list of _unique_ integral ( not necessarily non-negative ) numbers, determine the number that is _odder than the rest_.  
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Given the constraints, there will always be exactly one such number.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m # Examples
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
