[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Given a natural number n, calculate sum of all its proper divisors. A proper divisor of a natural number is the divisor that is strictly less than the number.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Example 1:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Input: n = 10
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Output: 8 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Explanation: Proper divisors 1 + 2 + 5 = 8. 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Example 2:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Input: n = 6
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Output: 6
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Explanation: Proper divisors 1 + 2 + 3 = 6. 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Your Task:  
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m You dont need to read input or print anything. Complete the function divSum() which takes n as input parameter and returns sum of proper divisors of n.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Expected Time Complexity: O(sqrt(n))
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Expected Auxiliary Space: O(1)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Constraints:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 2<= n <=10^{6} #User function Template for python3
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m class Solution:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     def divSum (self, n):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m         # code here \nUse Call-Based format
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m [code] def divSum(self, n):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     sum_divisors = 1  # 1 is always a proper divisor of any natural number
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m         if n % i == 0:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m             sum_divisors += i
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m             if i != n // i:  # Avoid adding the square root twice for perfect squares
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m                 sum_divisors += n // i
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     return sum_divisors
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
