[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m [question] QUESTION:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m A generalization of Bézier surfaces, called the S-patch, uses an interesting scheme for indexing its control points.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m In the case of an n-sided surface of degree d, each index has n non-negative integers that sum to d, and all possible configurations are used.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m For example, for a 3-sided quadratic (degree 2) surface the control points are:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m > indices 3 2 => [[0,0,2],[0,1,1],[0,2,0],[1,0,1],[1,1,0],[2,0,0]]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m Given the degree and the number of sides, generate all control point indices.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m The order of the indices in the list can be arbitrary, so for the above example
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m > [[1,1,0],[2,0,0],[0,0,2],[0,2,0],[0,1,1],[1,0,1]]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m is also a good solution. def indices(n, d):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 	\nUse Call-Based format
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m [code] from itertools import product
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m def indices(n, d):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     # Generate all possible d-element combinations of (n, 0, 1)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     combinations = list(set(map(tuple, product([0, 1] * n, repeat=d))))
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     # Sort the combinations by magnitude, then lexicographically
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     combinations.sort(key=lambda x: (sum(x), x))
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m     return combinations
[36m(TaskRunner pid=57070)[0m 
